$DIR/main.p:37:8: a is used here and moved by the same expression at column 17
    Buf is an owner type: its values own what they hold and release it when they
    are dropped. One expression or statement may not move a variable and also
    use it, in either order: whether the use sees the value would depend on the
    order the parts are evaluated in, and a use that runs after the move would
    find a holding nothing.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Do the use in a statement of its own before the one that moves a, and use v
    in its place:
        v := a.Len()
    (see "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3301" for a longer explanation)

